"H6927":"former","H2986":"to conduct","H7272":"foot","H7350":"distant","H1481":"to sojourn","H4310":"who?","H3289":"to advise","H5921":"upon","H5849":"to surround","H8269":"ruler","H3667":"Canaan","H3513":"honour","H3068":"LORD","H6635":"army","H2490":"to bore","H1347":"pride","H3605":"all","H6643":"beauty","H7043":"to lighten","H1323":"daughter","H369":"nothing","H4206":"belt","H5750":"still","H3027":"hand","H5186":"to stretch","H7264":"to tremble","H4467":"kingdom","H6680":"to command","H413":"to(wards)","H8045":"to destroy","H3254":"again","H5937":"to exult","H6231":"to oppress","H6965":"(Leb)-kamai","H1571":"also","H8033":"there","H5117":"to rest","H2005":"look!","H3778":"Chaldea","H2088":"this","H5971":"people","H804":"Assyria","H3245":"to found","H6728":"wild beast","H971":"tower","H6209":"to strip","H759":"palace","H7760":"make","H4654":"ruin","H1931":"he/she/it","H7911":"to forget","H7657":"seventy","H8141":"year","H4428":"king","H259":"one","H7093":"end","H7892":"song","H2181":"to fornicate","H3947":"take","H3658":"lyre","H5437":"turn","H5892":"excitement","H3190":"be good","H5059":"to play","H7235":"to multiply","H4616":"because","H2142":"to remember","H6485":"list","H853":"[Obj.]","H7725":"return","H868":"wages","H6440":"before","H127":"soil","H5504":"profit","H6944":"holiness","H686":"to store","H2630":"to hoard","H398":"to eat","H7654":"satiety","H4374":"covering","H6266":"surpassing"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Tyre was destroyed, in part, by Nabuchodonosor. Cyrus permitted all the captives of this, as well as of other countries, to return. — Cethim; Cyrus, or rather Macedonia. Merchants come thence no longer."],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Island. Tyre was originally surrounded with water. A communication with the land was made afterwards, Josue xix. 29. (Calmet) (Ezechiel xxvii.) (Worthington)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Nile. Hebrew Shichor, or “muddy water,” designates that river, Josue xiii. 3. — River. The overflowing of the Nile gave fertility to Egypt, insomuch that Tyre and other nations were supplied by it with corn."],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Strength: people who sail. Septuagint, “but the strength….replied: I,” &c. Sidon will not be concerned for the fall of her rival. She alleges that she has nothing to do with Tyre. That city would not allow that it was a colony of Sidon, ver. 12. (Calmet)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Seas. The rich Tyrians did so. (St. Jerome) — Septuagint, “to Carthage.” Hebrew Tharsis, in Cilicia. (Calmet) — Hence Nabuchodonosor did not find a sufficient reward, Ezechiel xxix. 18."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Sojourn. Many fled, others were made captives."],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Earth. The merchants were as rich as kings, or the latter sent their merchandise to Tyre, Ezechiel xxvii. 33."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Girdle, fortress; or rather, thou art naked, like a slave, chap. xx. 4."],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Daughter; colony. (Calmet) — Oppressed. Literally, “calumniated.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","It. Hebrew adds, “for fishermen.” It was formerly covered with water. (Eusebius, pręp. ix.) — Ruin. The fall of Babylon has been denounced, chap. xiii."],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","King Nabuchodonosor, whose two sons reigned but a short time. The captivity of the people of God began also the year of the world 3398, and ended the year of the world 3468, the first of Cyrus. — Harlot. She shall be re-established, (Calmet) and shall invite people to her markets. (Sanchez) — Before Cyrus, she had kings, but they were of small power. The city was become very rich, and well fortified, when Alexander [the Great] was stopped by it for seven months. See Ezechiel xxvi. 14. (Calmet) — Tyre had rejoiced at the misfortunes of the Hebrews, and was punished by the like captivity. (Worthington)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Commit. Septuagint, “be the emporium for all.” Great injustice prevailed formerly among merchants, so that it is represented as a dishonest calling. (Haydock)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Sanctified to the Lord. This alludes to the conversion of the Gentiles. (Challoner) — Before, the Tyrians were great enemies to the Jews, 2 Esdras xiii. 16., and 1 Machabees v. 15. Our Saviour wrought a miracle in favour of one of this country, Matthew v. 22., and Zacharias viii. 20. (Calmet) — Continuance. Literally, “old age.” Aquila, “with changes of dress.” (Haydock)"],["challoner",18,null,"challoner","Sanctified to the Lord.... This alludes to the conversion of the Gentiles."]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"]},"fa":[[4,null,0,"Morals on the Book of Job","Preface",0,604,"Divine Providence has compassed us about and cut off all excuse. All openings to people's equivocating arts are in every way closed. A Gentile, one without the law, is brought forward to confound the iniquity of those that are under the law; which is well and summarily shown by the prophet, when he says, \"Be ashamed, O Sidon, says the sea. For in Sidon we have a figure of the steadfastness of those settled upon the foundation of the law, and in the sea of the life of the Gentiles. …"]],"fau":[["gregory-great","Gregory the Great",1,"father",null,1,604]],"ftr":[["Library of the Fathers, Morals on the Book of Job (1844-1850)"]]}